{
  "passage": "Many people use the words breathing and respiration as if they mean the same thing, but they are different processes. Breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs. When we breathe in, the chest cavity expands and air rushes into the lungs. When we breathe out, the chest cavity becomes smaller and air is pushed out. Respiration is what happens inside the cells of the body. In respiration, glucose from our food is broken down to release energy, which the cells use for every activity, from moving a muscle to thinking. When oxygen is used, the glucose is broken down completely into carbon dioxide and water, and this is called aerobic respiration. When there is not enough oxygen, glucose is broken down only partly. This is anaerobic respiration. During hard exercise, our leg muscles may respire anaerobically and produce lactic acid, which causes cramps. Yeast also respires without oxygen and produces alcohol, which is why it is used in making bread and in brewing. Plants respire too, all the time, day and night, through tiny pores in their leaves and through their roots. It is a common mistake to think that plants respire only at night. During the day their respiration is simply hidden, because photosynthesis uses up more carbon dioxide than respiration releases.",
